      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely! Alveolar fricatives are produced by forcing air through a narrow channel formed by the tongue and the alveolar ridge. In the /z/ sound, your vocal cords vibrate, creating a buzzing effect, while in the /s/ sound, your vocal cords remain still, causing a hissing effect. The voiced /z/ is therefore a vibration or trill in your vocal cords, while the unvoiced /s/ is friction without vocal cord vibration.",
